# RFC 8288 Link response headers for agent / machine discovery.
# A single source honored by BOTH deploy targets: Astro copies this file from
# public/ into the published output (dist/client for the Cloudflare adapter,
# dist root for the Netlify adapter), and each platform's static asset handler
# applies it. Keep this as the only place these headers are defined so the two
# targets never drift.
# Relation types are IANA-registered and point to resources that exist:
#   describedby -> /llms.txt   (LLM-readable description of this site, RFC 8288)
#   service-doc -> /docs       (human-oriented documentation, RFC 8631)
/*
  Link: </llms.txt>; rel="describedby"; type="text/plain", </docs>; rel="service-doc"; type="text/html"

# Markdown twins. Every page is also published as agent-facing markdown at the
# same path with the trailing slash removed and ".md" appended:
#   /about/    -> /about.md
#   /docs/cli/ -> /docs/cli.md
#   /          -> /index.md
#
# Content-Type is pinned because Cloudflare serves .md as bare "text/markdown"
# with NO charset, and markdown has no in-band way to declare its encoding. The
# docs bodies contain real UTF-8 (arrows, typographic quotes), so without this a
# browser decodes them as Latin-1 and renders mojibake.
#
# Measured trade-off, not a guess: with this line, a request for a .md that does
# not exist returns HTTP 404 with an HTML body still labelled text/markdown,
# because Cloudflare matches these rules against the REQUEST path and applies
# them after resolving the 404 asset. That was verified on a preview deploy.
# Accepted, because the 404 status code is the primary signal and stays correct,
# whereas dropping this line mis-decodes all 232 valid markdown files. The clean
# fix for both is a markdown-aware 404, which needs a Worker.
#
# Cache-Control is safe under /*.md but must NEVER appear under /*: the
# Cloudflare adapter probes /_astro/probe against this file and skips injecting
# its own immutable cache block if any matching rule sets Cache-Control.
/*.md
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600
  Vary: Accept

# Per-page markdown alternate, as an HTTP header for clients that never parse
# the HTML. Cloudflare's rules engine compiles :name to a named capture and
# substitutes it into the value; Netlify treats :name as a path matcher only and
# would emit a literal "</:s1.md>", so these rules only pay off once Cloudflare
# is the single target. Until then the <link rel="alternate"> in SEO.astro is
# what carries this relation, and it reaches every page on both platforms.
#
# Every rule requires a trailing slash, which no asset URL has, so they cannot
# match /logo.png or /_astro/hash.css. Max route depth in this build is 3.
/
  Link: </index.md>; rel="alternate"; type="text/markdown"
/:s1/
  Link: </:s1.md>; rel="alternate"; type="text/markdown"
/:s1/:s2/
  Link: </:s1/:s2.md>; rel="alternate"; type="text/markdown"
/:s1/:s2/:s3/
  Link: </:s1/:s2/:s3.md>; rel="alternate"; type="text/markdown"

# The wildcard rules above cannot express the hasMarkdownTwin() guard that
# SEO.astro applies to the HTML <link>, so they would advertise a twin for the
# 13 /blueprints/page/N redirect stubs, which have none and would 404. Unset the
# header for them. These are noindex meta-refresh stubs that hand-roll their own
# document and never reach Layout, so they lose nothing else by it.
/blueprints/page/:n/
  ! Link
